Treat $y$ as a function of $x$. The outer product and the exponential chain rule both matter. First differentiate the exponential input:

$$
\begin{aligned}
\frac{d}{dx}\left(- x y\right)&=- \frac{dy}{dx} x - y\\
\frac{d}{dx}\left(e^{- x y}\right)&=\left(- \frac{dy}{dx} x - y\right) e^{- x y}
\end{aligned}
$$

Differentiate both sides with respect to $x$, then collect the derivative terms:

$$
\begin{aligned}
&\frac{d}{dx}\left(- x^{2} + y e^{- x y}\right)=\frac{d}{dx}\left(- e - 1\right)\\
&\frac{dy}{dx} e^{- x y} - 2 x + y \left(- \frac{dy}{dx} x - y\right) e^{- x y}=0\\
&\left(- x y e^{- x y} + e^{- x y}\right)\frac{dy}{dx}=-\left(- 2 x - y^{2} e^{- x y}\right)\\
&\frac{dy}{dx}=-\frac{- 2 x - y^{2} e^{- x y}}{- x y e^{- x y} + e^{- x y}}
\end{aligned}
$$

At $(1,-1)$, the coefficient of $\dfrac{dy}{dx}$ is $2 e\ne 0$. Substitute the point to obtain

$$
\begin{aligned}
m&=\left.\frac{dy}{dx}\right|_{(1,-1)}\\
&=-\frac{- e - 2}{2 e}\\
&=e^{-1} + \frac{1}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

